@startuml ota-update
' The OTA update state machine on the target.
' Grounded in Src/ota_update.c and in industrial-hmi
' docs/protocols/uart-flash-v1.md, sections 4 to 6 (AGREED).

title The update session on the board

skinparam shadowing false

[*] --> IDLE

state IDLE : normal mode, telemetry runs
state RECEIVING : between BEGIN and COMMIT\ntelemetry quiet
state COMMITTED : bank selected\nreset requested

IDLE --> RECEIVING : BEGIN, fits, erase OK\n/ ACK
RECEIVING --> RECEIVING : DATA at the expected offset\n/ program, ACK
RECEIVING --> RECEIVING : BEGIN again\n/ erase, start over, ACK
RECEIVING --> COMMITTED : COMMIT, all bytes in,\nCRC32 matches, bank selected\n/ ACK, THEN reset
RECEIVING --> IDLE : ABORT / ACK
RECEIVING --> IDLE : 10 s silent since\nthe board's last answer
RECEIVING --> IDLE : flash error / NAK FLASH_ERROR
RECEIVING --> IDLE : CRC32 differs / NAK VERIFY_FAILED
IDLE --> IDLE : INFO_REQ / INFO\nCONFIRM / ACK

note right of IDLE
  A frame with a bad CRC16 is ignored here,
  answered NAK BAD_CRC only in a session:
  outside one it is almost always line noise.
end note

note bottom of RECEIVING
  **Every message is checked in the same order:**
  1. shape: can it be understood?   NAK BAD_MESSAGE
  2. state: is it allowed now?      NAK BAD_STATE
  3. content: offset, size, CRC32   the specific NAK

  **A repeat does nothing twice.** The same TYPE and SEQ
  as the last frame answered OK gets that same answer
  again: no second erase, no second write. A NAK is never
  remembered, so a clean resend after NAK BAD_CRC is
  judged again and accepted.
end note

legend bottom
  Behind the port, so all of this runs on a PC: the flash is a RAM array,
  the clock a variable, the UART a capture buffer. 73 checks, 15 mutants.
  The silence counts from the board's LAST ANSWER, not from the last frame:
  the erase at BEGIN is the board's own work and must not count against
  the host (spec section 6, Timeouts).
endlegend

@enduml
